1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,825
Two hours from Agent 1, no longer hunting for the one email that matters in a pile

2
00:00:04,825 --> 00:00:05,316
of forty.

3
00:00:05,316 --> 00:00:09,846
About fifty minutes from Agent 2, one clean list per call instead of scrawled notes

4
00:00:09,846 --> 00:00:11,265
half-remembered by Friday.

5
00:00:11,265 --> 00:00:14,266
Forty minutes from Agent 3, the weekly arithmetic gone.

6
00:00:14,266 --> 00:00:18,850
Half an hour from Agent 4, chases that used to slip now sitting drafted and waiting.

7
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:20,542
Where did the figure come from?

8
00:00:20,542 --> 00:00:25,072
Six weeks of the C09 tracker, filled in weekly, checking time subtracted every row,

9
00:00:25,072 --> 00:00:26,982
exactly as the last lecture taught.

10
00:00:26,982 --> 00:00:31,566
That provenance is the difference between this slide and every breathless automation

11
00:00:31,566 --> 00:00:36,150
statistic you have ever scrolled past: you are looking at one person's counted week,

12
00:00:36,150 --> 00:00:40,570
not an industry's press release, and by the time you present your own version, it

13
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:42,098
will have the same pedigree.

14
00:00:42,098 --> 00:00:46,410
The six weeks were not flat, either, and the shape is worth knowing in advance.

15
00:00:46,410 --> 00:00:50,830
Week one barely broke even: trials were running, every output was being read, and

16
00:00:50,830 --> 00:00:53,068
the checking time ate most of the saving.

17
00:00:53,068 --> 00:00:57,379
The number grew as trust climbed the ladder, as briefings absorbed their tuning

18
00:00:57,379 --> 00:00:59,726
edits, and as checking thinned to sampling.

19
00:00:59,726 --> 00:01:04,310
If your own week two looks unimpressive, that is not failure, that is the curve, and

20
00:01:04,310 --> 00:01:08,839
it is precisely why the tracker runs for six weeks before anyone presents anything.

21
00:01:09,389 --> 00:01:12,638
Notice that table adds to four hours, not five and a half.

22
00:01:12,638 --> 00:01:17,286
The extra ninety minutes in Priya's real total came from something the tracker does

23
00:01:17,286 --> 00:01:21,767
not have a row for: fewer things falling through the cracks altogether, and less

24
00:01:21,767 --> 00:01:25,687
time spent each week simply worrying about what she might have missed.

25
00:01:25,687 --> 00:01:30,168
That number is real but softer, and the honest thing to do is name it separately

26
00:01:30,168 --> 00:01:32,408
rather than fold it into the hard total.

27
00:01:32,408 --> 00:01:37,113
Read the ranking in the table too, because it teaches you where to start in your own

28
00:01:37,113 --> 00:01:37,337
job.

29
00:01:37,337 --> 00:01:41,761
Triage wins by a distance, and not because it is the cleverest agent: it is the

30
00:01:41,761 --> 00:01:42,265
simplest.

31
00:01:42,265 --> 00:01:43,441
It wins on frequency.

32
00:01:43,441 --> 00:01:47,922
Forty one emails a day, every day, each shaving a sliver of attention, compounds

33
00:01:47,922 --> 00:01:52,234
past anything a weekly task can offer, however painful the weekly task feels.

34
00:01:52,234 --> 00:01:56,771
When you pick your own first agent after this course, resist the dramatic monthly

35
00:01:56,771 --> 00:01:59,011
chore and automate the boring daily one.

36
00:01:59,011 --> 00:02:03,436
The maths is on the boring one's side, and the daily agent also earns its trial

37
00:02:03,436 --> 00:02:07,020
evidence four times faster, which Section 7 taught you to value.

38
00:02:07,570 --> 00:02:12,218
This is the same instinct as Section 7's spot checks, aimed at a spreadsheet instead

39
00:02:12,218 --> 00:02:16,865
of an agent's output: check your own numbers as carefully as you would check theirs.

40
00:02:16,865 --> 00:02:20,075
And when someone hears "four hours" and asks, is that all?

41
00:02:20,075 --> 00:02:24,501
Give the answer Priya gives Marcus in the next section: four hours a week is two

42
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:28,761
hundred hours a year, five full working weeks, bought back for the price of a

43
00:02:28,761 --> 00:02:32,801
sandwich a month, counted by someone who subtracts her own checking time.

44
00:02:32,801 --> 00:02:37,061
Said that way, the modest number stops sounding modest, and it still contains

45
00:02:37,061 --> 00:02:38,721
nothing anyone can take apart.

46
00:02:38,721 --> 00:02:41,155
Big claims impress until the first question.

47
00:02:41,155 --> 00:02:42,871
Clean claims survive the tenth.

48
00:02:43,421 --> 00:02:47,870
Four hours a week is real money and real relief, once you have counted your own the

49
00:02:47,870 --> 00:02:48,728
same honest way.

50
00:02:48,728 --> 00:02:52,963
And if your version of the table adds to ninety minutes rather than four hours,

51
00:02:52,963 --> 00:02:57,252
present it with the same straight face: an hour and a half a week is most of two

52
00:02:57,252 --> 00:03:01,702
working weeks a year, cleanly counted, for pocket change, and a small honest number

53
00:03:01,702 --> 00:03:03,953
opens more doors than a large fragile one.

54
00:03:03,953 --> 00:03:08,403
It was never going to be everything, and the next lecture is about the part of your

55
00:03:08,403 --> 00:03:11,191
own job that was never meant to be automated at all.
